#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142606 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142982 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbee94b0-ad82-5461-81f0-84237abf0c85" :knowledge-point/title "Dividing Unit Fractions"}] :question/id #uuid "7e77df17-125b-5a13-9429-dc1fdc15cb26" :question/math-academy-id "e-13780" :question/problem "Find the value of $\\dfrac{1}{4} \\div \\dfrac{1}{8}$." :question/worked-solution "Dividing a fraction by another fraction is equivalent to multiplying the first fraction by the reciprocal of the second fraction.\n\nThe reciprocal of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}1}{\\color{red}8}\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}8}{\\color{blue}1}\n$$\n\nSo, we have:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 4 \\div \\dfrac{\\color{blue}1}{\\color{red}8} = \\dfrac 1 4 \\times \\dfrac{\\color{red}8}{\\color{blue}1}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply fractions. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 {4} \\times \\dfrac{8}{1} = \\dfrac {1\\times 8} {4\\times 1} = \\dfrac{8}{4}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify:\n$8\\div 4=2$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4} \\div \\dfrac{1}{8} = 2 \\,\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142607 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142983 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "008c73e1-62d2-51aa-a937-40a810d7d594" :knowledge-point/title "Dividing Fractions When Simplification Is Needed: Finding a Missing Number"}] :question/id #uuid "ff77c815-b89c-5157-9c1c-cf52bddda0fb" :question/math-academy-id "e-13781" :question/problem "What is the missing digit in the following equality?\n$0$" :question/worked-solution "Dividing a fraction by another fraction is equivalent to multiplying the first fraction by the reciprocal of the second fraction.\n\nThe reciprocal of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}4}{\\color{red}3}\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}3}{\\color{blue}4}\n$$\n\nSo, we have:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 6 {7} \\div \\dfrac{\\color{blue}4}{\\color{red}3} = \\dfrac 6 {7} \\times \\dfrac{\\color{red}3}{\\color{blue}4}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply fractions. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 6 {7} \\times \\dfrac{3}{4} = \\dfrac {6\\times 3} {7\\times 4} = \\dfrac{18}{28}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{18}{28} = \\dfrac{18\\div 2}{28\\div 2} = \\dfrac{\\color{blue}9}{14}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 6 {7}\\div \\dfrac 4 {3} = \\dfrac{\\color{blue}9}{14} \\,\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the missing number is ${\\color{blue}{9}}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142608 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142984 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0c20028f-c518-5353-9d1d-f69a7a62a0a8" :knowledge-point/title "Dividing Fractions When Simplification Is Needed"}] :question/id #uuid "4597d0ec-e04c-581a-ad9f-e78280182444" :question/math-academy-id "e-13784" :question/problem "Calculate the value of $\\dfrac{1}{12} \\div \\dfrac{2}{3}$." :question/worked-solution "Dividing a fraction by another fraction is equivalent to multiplying the first fraction by the reciprocal of the second fraction.\n\nThe reciprocal of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}2}{\\color{red}3}\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}3}{\\color{blue}2}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 {12} \\div \\dfrac{\\color{blue}2}{\\color{red}3} = \\dfrac 1 {12} \\times \\dfrac{\\color{red}3}{\\color{blue}2}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply the fractions. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 {12} \\times \\dfrac{3}{2} = \\dfrac {1\\times 3} {12\\times 2} = \\dfrac{3}{24}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{24} = \\dfrac{3\\div 3}{24\\div 3} = \\dfrac{1}{8}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{12} \\div \\dfrac{2}{3} = \\dfrac{1}{8} \\,\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142609 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142985 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3260562-39ce-5ec6-a367-679f736dd673" :knowledge-point/title "Dividing Fractions When the Result Is a Mixed Number"}] :question/id #uuid "d361a96a-7abd-50a3-801d-9eb26f0c119c" :question/math-academy-id "e-13786" :question/problem "Calculate the value of $\\dfrac{1}{3} \\div \\dfrac 2 {13}$." :question/worked-solution "Dividing a fraction by another fraction is equivalent to multiplying the first fraction by the reciprocal of the second fraction.\n\nThe reciprocal of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}2}{\\color{red}13}\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}13}{\\color{blue}2}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 {3} \\div \\dfrac{\\color{blue}2}{\\color{red}13} = \\dfrac 1 {3} \\times \\dfrac{\\color{red}13}{\\color{blue}2}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply fractions. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 {3} \\times \\dfrac{13}{2} = \\dfrac {1\\times 13} {3 \\times 2} = \\dfrac{13}{6}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{13}{6} =2\\,\\textrm{R} 1 = 2\\,\\dfrac 1 {6}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{3} \\div \\dfrac 2 {13} = 2\\,\\dfrac 1 {6} \\,\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143316 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142982 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbee94b0-ad82-5461-81f0-84237abf0c85" :knowledge-point/title "Dividing Unit Fractions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a36ee1d9-9efc-5ce0-99bf-a49f7a1fd5d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186140866} {:answer/id #uuid "2fee9a96-77fd-5020-ae63-a9c24b1332d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186140867} {:answer/id #uuid "3a58237e-e03a-53c8-8d11-c00f4b60587f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186140868} {:answer/id #uuid "441375db-0f22-56d0-8dd3-d3f0906a55e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186140869} {:answer/id #uuid "6272b